      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          InnoSupps Advanced Iso is a decent naturally sweetened whey isolate with 25 g of protein per scoop, but at $54.99 for 25 servings on Amazon it costs about $2.20 per 25 g, more than Transparent Labs or Dymatize ISO100. A subscription price on InnoSupps.com changes the math. Below: the label, the MCT and casein surprise, testing, cost and better-value swaps.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. InnoSupps Advanced Iso Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            InnoSupps Advanced Iso gives 25 g of protein per scoop from whey protein isolate, sweetened with monk fruit and stevia rather than sucralose, per the label at the time of writing. It is a reasonable product with an unreasonable full price: $54.99 for 25 servings on Amazon is about $2.20 per 25 g of protein, and the brand does not hold a sport certification we could find.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want dessert-style flavors without artificial sweeteners and can get the $44.99 subscription price on InnoSupps.com, which brings it to about $1.80 per 25 g. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you are drug tested, want the leanest isolate possible, or pay full price, because <a href="/transparent-labs-protein-powder-review/">Transparent Labs</a> and <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize ISO100</a> both cost less per gram and carry a named testing program.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Question</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Answer (per label or listing, Nov 2026)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g from instantized whey isolate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140-160, varies by flavor</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Monk fruit extract + enzymatically modified stevia</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Servings per tub</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Amazon price seen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$54.99 (Hot Cocoa Crunch)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.20 on Amazon; about $1.80 on brand subscription</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sport certification</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None found (NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Advanced Iso and the Amazon Alternatives Worth Comparing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Advanced Iso first, then three Amazon isolates that beat it on price, testing or both, and InnoSupps' own plant protein. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 16 November 2026; cost per 25 g uses the servings and protein printed on each label or listing.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in InnoSupps Advanced Iso?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Advanced Iso contains instantized whey protein isolate, a medium-chain triglyceride (MCT) powder, organic agave inulin, natural flavors, pink Himalayan salt, guar and xanthan gum, monk fruit extract and enzymatically modified stevia, per the ingredient panel InnoSupps shows on its product page. Chocolate-style flavors add alkalized cocoa powder. The allergen line reads "Contains Milk".
          </p>

          <p>
            The nutrition panels InnoSupps publishes for its flavors show 25 g of protein in a scoop of roughly 35.3 to 36.5 g, which means protein is about 68 to 71% of the scoop by weight. Calories run from 140 for the Fruity Cereal Donut panel to 160 for Caramel Coconut Cookie. Total carbohydrate is 3 to 4 g, of which 1 to 2 g is fiber from the agave inulin, and sugar is listed at 0 g. Sodium is 230 mg per scoop. InnoSupps also says each serving holds more than 5 g of branched-chain amino acids, which is normal for 25 g of whey.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does an isolate have 3.5 g of saturated fat?</h3>

          <p>
            The fat comes from the MCT powder, not the whey. The panels list 3 to 3.5 g of total fat, all of it saturated, which is 15 to 18% of the daily value per scoop. For comparison, most plain whey isolates, including Dymatize ISO100 and Isopure Zero Carb, land around 0.5 to 1 g of fat. MCTs are made from palm kernel and coconut oil, and InnoSupps likely adds them for a creamier texture. That is a fair choice for taste, but it explains why Advanced Iso has 140 to 160 calories when 25 g of protein alone accounts for about 100.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is Advanced Iso really 100% whey isolate?</h3>

          <p>
            The protein source is whey isolate, but the powder is not only whey. The MCT powder is itself a blend of medium-chain triglycerides, sodium caseinate (a milk protein), silicon dioxide and sunflower lecithin, per the ingredient panel. Sodium caseinate is used as a carrier to turn oil into a dry powder, and the amount is likely small, but it does mean there is some casein in the tub. The "100% Whey Isolate" in the Amazon title refers to the protein ingredient, not to everything in the scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more label detail matters for anyone with an allergy. The InnoSupps graphic shows a "Dairy/Lactose Free" icon next to a panel that says "Contains Milk". Whey isolate is very low in lactose and many lactose-intolerant people handle it well, but it is a dairy protein. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid Advanced Iso entirely and look at the brand's pea-based Clean Vegan Protein or our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein guide</a>. Labels can change, so check the panel on your tub before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweetener-flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is InnoSupps Advanced Iso Sweetened With?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Advanced Iso is sweetened with monk fruit extract and enzymatically modified stevia, per its ingredient panel, and InnoSupps says none of its products contain sucralose or other artificial sweeteners. The organic agave inulin adds a little sweetness and fiber too, but no sugar shows on the panel. That makes Advanced Iso one of the few dessert-flavored isolates that avoids sucralose and acesulfame potassium, the pair used by most big-brand wheys.
          </p>

          <p>
            Enzymatically modified stevia is stevia leaf extract treated with enzymes to reduce the bitter aftertaste that plain stevia can leave. It still tastes like stevia to some people, and that matters if you are among them. If stevia is the problem rather than sucralose, Advanced Iso is not your powder, and our guide to the <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/">best protein powder without stevia</a> covers the alternatives. If you avoid artificial sweeteners for any reason, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">no-artificial-sweetener roundup</a> compares Advanced Iso's rivals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which flavors does Advanced Iso come in?</h3>

          <p>
            InnoSupps.com listed five Advanced Iso flavors when we checked: Buttery Pancakes, Caramel Coconut Cookie, Hot Cocoa Crunch, Vanilla Ice Cream and Fruity Cereal Donut. Amazon carried Hot Cocoa Crunch, Caramel Coconut Cookie and Buttery Pancake at $54.99 each. There is no unflavored version, so everyone gets the sweetener, and the cereal and pancake flavors are clearly built for drinking on their own rather than blending into a smoothie with fruit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories differ slightly between flavors because of the cocoa and flavor systems. The Caramel Coconut Cookie panel lists 160 calories, 4 g of carbohydrate and 2 g of fiber; Fruity Cereal Donut lists 140 calories and 3 g of carbohydrate. If you are counting tightly, read the panel for the exact flavor you buy, since we could not find a published panel for Hot Cocoa Crunch on the brand's site.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is InnoSupps Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            InnoSupps says every product "undergoes vigorous, NSF-certified third-party testing" and is made in a GMP-certified facility in the United States, per the FAQ on its Advanced Iso page. What it does not claim is a product certification: we found no InnoSupps product in NSF's Certified for Sport search when we checked, and InnoSupps does not mention Informed Sport or Informed Choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters. "NSF-certified testing" most likely means a lab or facility holds an NSF-related accreditation, which is a quality step, but it is not the same as the product carrying the NSF Certified for Sport mark. That mark means each lot is screened for more than 280 banned substances and that the label contents are verified by NSF itself, with the result listed in a public database. Informed Sport works the same way. Brands that hold those marks almost always print the logo and name the program.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also could not find a published certificate of analysis or heavy-metal results for Advanced Iso on the InnoSupps site. Some brands, Transparent Labs among them, post batch results; InnoSupps does not appear to. That does not mean Advanced Iso is contaminated. Whey isolate generally tests low for lead and arsenic compared with plant and chocolate proteins, as our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> explains, but you are taking the brand's word for it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TESTING RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Drug-tested athletes should skip Advanced Iso.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your sport tests for banned substances, buy a protein listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases. Dymatize ISO100 and Transparent Labs both promote Informed Choice; Thorne Whey Isolate is NSF Certified for Sport. InnoSupps has no such listing we could find.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the practical risk is low. Advanced Iso is a whey protein made in the US by a brand with a large customer base, and it uses common, well-studied ingredients. The gap is in verification, not in any known problem. If independent testing is one of your reasons for paying a premium, though, Advanced Iso does not give you that premium's usual return.
          </p>
        </div>

