        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy InnoSupps Advanced Iso, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Advanced Iso suits people who want a sweet, dessert-flavored whey isolate without sucralose and who will buy it on subscription or in a bundle. At about $1.60 to $1.80 per 25 g on those terms, it is competitive, and the flavor-first formula makes it easy to drink with plain water, which is where many naturally sweetened powders fall down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are a drug-tested athlete, since there is no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing. Skip it if you are cutting hard and counting every calorie, because the MCT powder adds 40 to 60 calories and 3 to 3.5 g of saturated fat per scoop compared with a plain isolate. Skip it if you have a milk allergy, despite the dairy-free icon on the brand's graphic. And skip it at the full $54.99 to $59.99 price, where Transparent Labs gives you more protein and a certification for less per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much protein you need determines how fast a tub goes. Most active adults do well on about 1.2 to 2.0 g of protein per kilogram of body weight a day from all sources, and one 25 g shake is a supplement to meals, not a replacement. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> has the numbers by body weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Health note: whey protein is safe for most healthy adults, but people with kidney disease, those who are pregnant or breastfeeding, anyone with a milk allergy and anyone taking medication or managing a medical condition should talk with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a protein supplement. See our overview of <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> for the evidence.
          </p>
        </div>

