            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading "100% whey isolate" as "nothing else"</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The protein comes from whey isolate, but Advanced Iso also contains an MCT powder made with sodium caseinate, plus inulin, gums and sweeteners. That is normal for a flavored powder, but if you need pure whey isolate for an allergy plan or a strict diet, read the full ingredient panel, not the headline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the dairy-free icon with a milk allergy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The InnoSupps graphic shows a dairy and lactose-free icon, yet the panel says Contains Milk. Whey isolate is low in lactose, which helps people who are lactose intolerant, but it is still a milk protein. Anyone with a true milk allergy should choose a plant protein instead, and confirm with their allergist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying the full one-time price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At $59.99 on the brand site or $54.99 on Amazon, Advanced Iso costs $2.20 to $2.40 per 25 g, more than certified rivals. The subscription or bundle brings it to about $1.60 to $1.80. If you like it, buy it on those terms, and set a reminder so a 28-day subscription does not outpace your use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming lab tested means certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                InnoSupps says its products get NSF-certified third-party testing, but no Advanced Iso listing appears in NSF Certified for Sport, and the brand does not claim Informed Sport. For drug-tested athletes only a listed product counts. Check the certifier's public database yourself rather than relying on wording on a product page.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the extra calories on a cut</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Advanced Iso has 140 to 160 calories per scoop because of the MCT fat, about 40 to 60 more than a lean isolate such as ISO100 or Isopure Zero Carb. Two shakes a day adds up to around 100 calories. That is fine for maintenance, but on a tight cut a leaner isolate leaves more room for food.
              </p>
